{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>its fat, the tail whole</b> The term חֵלֶב (ḥelev, \"fat\") in the language of the Sanctuary denotes separated fat — that is, fat that is distinct and does not form a single entity with the flesh. For fat that is intermingled with the flesh and not separated from it is called שֻׁמָּן (shuman, \"grease/fatty\"), as in the verses: \"and Jeshurun grew fat\" (Deuteronomy 32:15), \"and they ate and were satisfied and grew fat\" (Nehemiah 9:25), \"make the heart of this people fat\" (Isaiah 6:10), \"and my flesh fails from fatness\" (Psalms 109:24), \"and it shall be fat and plump\" (Isaiah 30:23), \"my soul is satisfied as with fat and fatness\" (Psalms 63:6), and so in every place. And the חֵלֶב is that which is separated, over which there is a membrane and which is peeled away; the term does not apply to fat that is intermingled with flesh anywhere, as we say: \"fatty flesh\" (בָּשָׂר שָׁמֵן), not \"fatty-fat flesh\" (בְּשַׂר חֵלֶב), and likewise in other languages of the nations their names for these two are distinct.\n\nAnd the verse uses the term figuratively: \"when you lift up its fat from it\" (Numbers 18:30), for the best part that is lifted from the produce he calls by the name חֵלֶב, just as the fat is lifted up in the offerings — \"fat of kidneys of wheat\" (Deuteronomy 32:14), likening the wheat to the kidneys and their fat, just as he says \"and the blood of grapes you shall drink, wine\" (ibid.), and wine is not blood; \"and you shall eat the fat of the land\" (Genesis 45:18), meaning they shall eat all the fat of ox and sheep and goat and all the living creatures — by way of such a metaphor.\n\nNow, there is no חֵלֶב in the tail at all, but it does contain non-separated fat (שֻׁמָּן), just as there is in any good cut, such as the thigh and the shoulder. And physicians know by nature that חֵלֶב is never found close to the skin, nor in a limb that moves constantly. And they say that the nature of non-separated fat in the ribs and the sides is hot and moist, and in the tail it is hot and moist, whereas the separated fat — such as that upon the kidneys — is cold and moist, coarse and thick, difficult to digest, quick to be consumed, and it produces a white phlegm that is obstructive.\n\nIf so, when the verse states (Leviticus 3:17): \"all fat and all blood you shall not eat,\" the fat (שֻׁמָּן) upon the tail is not included, for it is not חֵלֶב in its name or in its nature. And if all non-separated fat were חֵלֶב, then all the fat in the shoulders and sides of the animal would be forbidden — for the verse did not say \"all the fat that they offer to YHWH you shall not eat,\" but rather said that for any animal that is offered upon the altar, all the חֵלֶב in it is forbidden. And it cannot be said that it forbade only what is offered from the animal, for if so the kidneys and the appendage on the liver would be forbidden. Rather, everything called חֵלֶב is forbidden even though it is not offered on the altar, such as the fat that is on the spleen; and that which is not called חֵלֶב is permitted even though it is offered, such as the kidneys and the appendage of the liver, and likewise the tail.\n\nThus the verse states regarding the ordination, in the command: \"and you shall take from the ram the fat and the tail\" (Exodus 29:22), and in the fulfillment: \"and he took the fat and the tail\" (Leviticus 8:25), for the tail is not fat. And this verse that says \"its fat, the tail whole\" — its meaning is that he shall offer from it its fat along with the whole tail, for when he removes the whole tail down to the bone, a great amount of fat that is attached to it on the inner side is removed along with it. And so they said in Torat Kohanim (Leviticus 19:2): \"to bring the fat that is adjacent to the tail\" — this is the fat that is between the vertebrae.\n\nAnd in my opinion the construal of this verse is like that of the other verse (Leviticus 7:3): \"and all its fat he shall offer from it, the tail\" — so here too it says: \"and he shall offer from the sacrifice of peace-offerings, a fire-offering to YHWH, all its fat\"; in both verses it first includes that he shall offer all its fat, and afterward details and mentions everything he offers from it — and not all of the items detailed are חֵלֶב, for the two kidneys and the appendage on the liver are not included in חֵלֶב at all. And thus it says in the section \"On the Eighth Day\" (Leviticus 9:10): \"and the fat and the kidneys and the appendage of the liver\" — it mentioned the fat alone and the rest of the entrails alone, just as it says (Exodus 29:22): \"from the ram, the fat and the tail\"; if so, the tail too is not offered under the category of its fat, but rather it said that he shall offer from the sacrifice of peace-offerings all its fat, and afterward detailed all the entrails that are fat and those that are not fat.\n\nAnd the verse that says concerning the sin-offering (Leviticus 4:31): \"and all its fat he shall remove, as the fat is removed from the sacrifice of peace-offerings\" — its meaning is not concerning the fat alone, for if so the kidneys and the appendage on the liver would not be offered in the sin-offering. Rather, its meaning is that he shall remove the fat along with everything that is removed together with it in the sacrifice of peace-offerings. And likewise: \"and all its fat he shall remove, as the fat of the sheep is removed from the sacrifice of peace-offerings\" (ibid. v. 35) — he shall remove it as it is removed in the sacrifice of peace-offerings along with everything removed there, so as to include the tail along with its fat. And likewise: \"and they placed the fats on the breasts and he burned the fats on the altar\" (Leviticus 9:20) — along with everything that was removed with them.\n\nAnd I have needed to elaborate on this to stop the mouths of the Sadducees, may their name be blotted out, for regarding words of Torah it is said: \"Answer a fool according to his folly\" (Proverbs 26:5), and they said (Avot 2:19): \"Be diligent to learn Torah, so that you may answer the heretic.\" And the Gaon Rav Saadya fled from them, for he said that in \"its fat, the tail\" a vav is missing, [and it should read] \"and the tail.\" And R. Abraham [Ibn Ezra] responded (on Leviticus 7:20) that it should have been \"the fat of its tail\" or \"the fat, the tail,\" but that is not a valid answer, for we find: \"and all Israel and its elders and officers and judges\" (Joshua 8:33). And further on (on Leviticus 7:25) I shall mention a great error that R. Abraham made in his arguments against them — it was harmful for him to speak against them. But the explanation of the Gaon is not correct, and all the more so is it not necessary in order to permit it [i.e., the fat of the tail].\n\nAnd the midrash that our Rabbis made (Ḥullin 117a) on \"all the fat of ox and sheep and goat\" (Leviticus 7:23) — that it forbade only the fat that is common to all of them — is a fitting midrash; but because it does not leave an opening for the litigant to argue, we have needed the proofs and arguments that we have written."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The citations to Ibn Ezra reference his commentary on Leviticus; the verse numbering 7:20 and 7:25 follows the Hebrew text as Ramban cites it, which may differ slightly from modern chapter-verse divisions of Ibn Ezra's commentary. פקוקלות (pekaklot) in the Torat Kohanim citation is a variant term for the tail vertebrae; translated hyper-literally as 'vertebrae' based on context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:51:02.835917162Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}